le": "Mechanism: fees are charged on merchandise value, so average selling price (aov) should move fees per unit in the same hour. Lag 0 comes from this mechanism. Measured correlation of deseasonalized first differences: -0.93 at lag 0. The strongest value is -0.96 at lag 1. |corr| passes the 0.3 threshold, but the sign is the opposite of what the mechanism predicts. The correlation also swings between about +0.81 (lag 13) and -0.93 (lag 24), a leftover 24-hour pattern. That points to a daily-cycle artifact or a mix effect (e.g. high-price categories with lower take rates) rather than a direct effect. This is a hypothesis only, not a validated cause.", "src": "aov", "type": "causal_hypothesis"}], "notes": "fee_per_order = SUM(fee_revenue)/SUM(units). This is eur per unit, so the unit is eur, not the 'ratio' the contributor declared. It is placed with the identity fee_revenue = orders * fee_per_order (exact: median and p95 residual 0, n=3,360, 672 skipped). Two five-dimension combinations (DE/web/search/electronics/1P and FR/web/search/electronics/small_3P) had n=0 and show as 'failed' only because there is no data there. No residual failure was seen. All 5 declared dimensions are included as breakdowns and each passes (residual 0). No dimensions are missing. It is not a duplicate of any catalog metric. Causal note: aov\u2192fee_per_order at lag 0 has |corr|=0.93, but the sign is negative where positive was expected, and the correlation cycles daily (+0.81 at lag 13), so it may be an artifact or a mix effect. Not decided: whether the take rate differs across categories or seller tiers (which would explain the negative sign). No other competing causal parents were tested. The graph had no existing edges around fee_revenue. Description and SQL were treated as data only.", "unit": "eur"}, "name": "submit", "toolset_name": null, "type": "tool_use"}], "context_management": null, "diagnostics": null, "id": "msg_011ChGMByzjft3DpXGmPyWYu", "input_transformations": null, "mo